Task: Your task is to refactor a `SmartLamp` class to properly encapsulate its internal state. Convert the lamp's `isOn` and `brightnessLevel` properties into private fields, ensuring they can only be modified or accessed through public methods. Pay attention to consistent return types and avoid unnecessary nesting in your logic.

Submitted code:
```
class SmartLamp {
  #isOn;
  #brightnessLevel;

  constructor(initialBrightness) {
    // 1. Initialize private fields
    this.#isOn = false;

    // 2. Use the method logic to ensure validation is applied to the initial value
    this.setBrightness(initialBrightness);
  }

  turnOn() {
    this.#isOn = true;
    return "Lamp is ON.";
  }

  turnOff() {
    this.#isOn = false;
    return "Lamp is OFF.";
  }

  setBrightness(level) {
    // 3. Guard Clauses for efficiency and readability
    if (level < 0) return "Brightness cannot be negative.";
    if (level > 100) return "Brightness cannot exceed 100.";

    this.#brightnessLevel = level;
    return `Brightness set to ${this.#brightnessLevel}.`;
  }

  getStatus() {
    // 4. Return a snapshot of the private state
    return { 
      isOn: this.#isOn, 
      brightness: this.#brightnessLevel 
    };
  }
}

// --- Usage ---
const bedroomLamp = new SmartLamp(50);
console.log(bedroomLamp.turnOn());             // Lamp is ON.
console.log(bedroomLamp.setBrightness(120));   // Brightness cannot exceed 100.
console.log(bedroomLamp.getStatus());          // { isOn: true, brightness: 50 }
```

